  "answer_text": "If you are completely unable to pass urine and are experiencing severe discomfort or pain, this is **acute urinary retention**. This is a **medical emergency**, and you should go to the nearest emergency room immediately to have your bladder safely drained and prevent damage to your kidneys. \n\nIf you are able to urinate but feel like your bladder does not empty all the way, you may be experiencing **chronic urinary retention**. This is a very common issue that can stem from several underlying causes, which generally fall into a few key categories:\n\n### 1. Physical Blockages (Obstructions)\nSomething may be physically restricting the path urine takes to leave your body:\n*   **Enlarged Prostate (BPH):** In men, the prostate gland naturally grows with age. Because it surrounds the urethra (the tube that carries urine out), an enlarged prostate can squeeze the tube and block the flow. This is the most common cause of retention in men.\n*   **Pelvic Organ Prolapse:** In women, weakened pelvic muscles can cause the bladder (cystocele) or uterus to sag and press against the urethra, pinching it closed.\n*   **Urethral Strictures:** Scar tissue inside the urethra\u2014often from past infections, surgeries, or catheter use\u2014can narrow the passage.\n*   **Bladder Stones:** Hard mineral deposits can form in the bladder and block the opening to the urethra.\n*   **Severe Constipation:** If the rectum is packed with hard stool, it can physically press against the bladder and urethra, preventing normal emptying.\n\n### 2. Nerve and Brain Signaling Issues\nFor your bladder to empty, your brain must signal the bladder muscle to squeeze while simultaneously telling the urinary sphincter (the valve that holds urine in) to relax. If the nerves carrying these signals are damaged or disrupted, the bladder cannot empty properly:\n*   **Chronic Diseases:** Conditions like diabetes (which can cause diabetic nerve damage), Multiple Sclerosis (MS), Parkinson\u2019s disease, or a past stroke.\n*   **Spinal Cord Injuries:** Herniated discs in the back, spinal trauma, or pelvic nerve injuries from surgery.\n*   **Anesthesia:** It is very common to experience temporary urinary retention right after a surgical procedure because anesthesia temporarily dulls the nerves that control the bladder.\n\n### 3. Medication Side Effects\nMany common medications can interfere with the nerve signals to your bladder, causing the bladder muscle to relax too much or preventing it from contracting:\n*   **Antihistamines** (allergy medications).\n*   **Decongestants** (cold medicines containing pseudoephedrine).\n*   **Tricyclic Antidepressants**.\n*   **Opioid Pain Relievers**.\n*   **Muscle Relaxants** or certain medications prescribed for overactive bladder.\n\n### 4. Infection or Inflammation\nSwelling in the urinary tract can compress the urethra and block the flow of urine:\n*   **Urinary Tract Infections (UTIs)** or urethritis.\n*   **Prostatitis** (inflammation or infection of the prostate in men).\n\n### 5. Weakened Bladder Muscles\nOver time, the bladder's main squeezing muscle (the detrusor muscle) can weaken. This can happen naturally with age, as a result of long-term obstruction, or if the bladder has been chronically overstretched.\n\n---\n\n### What You Should Do Next\n\nBecause leaving chronic urinary retention untreated can eventually lead to urinary tract infections, bladder damage, or kidney issues, it is important to have this evaluated by a primary care physician or a urologist. \n\nTo get the most out of your doctor's visit, you can take a few proactive steps to prepare:\n1.  **Keep a Bladder Diary:** For 2 to 3 days, write down what you drink, how often you urinate, and whether you feel like your bladder emptied completely each time. Note any leaking or difficulty starting your stream.\n2.  **Bring a Complete Medication List:** Include all prescription drugs, over-the-counter medications, and herbal supplements, as one of them could be contributing to the problem.\n3.  **Prepare Questions for Your Doctor:** \n    *   *\"Could any of my current medications be causing this?\"*\n    *   *\"Would a Post-Void Residual (PVR) test be helpful?\"* (This is a quick, painless ultrasound done right after you pee to measure exactly how much urine is left behind.)\n    *   *\"Do I need to see a specialist, like a urologist, to look for a physical blockage?\"*",
